body:
`fetchData` now reads `$expand` from `objectSchemaRef.current`, but this effect still runs immediately on mount, before `getObjectSchema()` resolves and populates the ref. Because `objectSchema` is no longer a dependency, the query will typically run with `expand=[]` and never re-fetch with lookup/master_detail expansions, which can be a functional regression for views that rely on expanded lookup objects. To keep the “single fetch” behavior without losing `$expand`, consider delaying `fetchData` until `objectSchemaRef.current?.fields` is available (or fetching schema inside `fetchData` when needed) so the first/only query includes the correct `$expand`.
```suggestion
        // If the schema has not been loaded yet, load it here so the first (and only)
        // data fetch can include the correct $expand configuration.
        let schemaForExpand = (objectSchemaRef.current as any) ?? null;
        if (!schemaForExpand || !schemaForExpand.fields) {
          try {
            const loadedSchema = await getObjectSchema();
            if (loadedSchema) {
              schemaForExpand = loadedSchema as any;
              objectSchemaRef.current = loadedSchema as any;
            }
          } catch {
            // If schema loading fails, fall back to a fetch without $expand.
          }
        }
        const expand = buildExpandFields(schemaForExpand?.fields);
```
